These patterns (a formula, or an `Outcome` evaluated against `data=` at fit time) are the only
interface you need for the entire package.

## Choosing a backend

The `load_dataset()` function is itself dataframe-agnostic. With its default it returns a Polars
data frame when Polars is installed, falls back to Pandas when only Pandas is available, and raises
a clear error if neither is. When you want a specific type, name it with the `backend=` argument,
which also accepts `"pyarrow"` for a PyArrow `Table`.

::: {#283da468 .cell execution_count=5}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load the same dataset explicitly in each backend
lung_pd = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="pandas")
lung_pl = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="polars")
lung_pa = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="pyarrow")

# Confirm each object belongs to a different library
[type(frame).__module__.split(".")[0] for frame in (lung_pd, lung_pl, lung_pa)]
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=5}
```
['pandas', 'polars', 'pyarrow']
```
:::
:::


The backend makes no difference to any result. The same model fit on any supported frame returns
the same coefficients, a property the test suite checks on every release. To show this, we fit one
formula to the same data held five ways: pandas, Polars, a PyArrow table, a DuckDB relation, and a
Polars `LazyFrame`. Because the formula names columns rather than using backend-specific
expressions, the call is identical for all of them.

::: {#95ffd9be .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import duckdb

# The same data in five forms (a SQL query over a Parquet file works like the DuckDB relation)
frames = {
    "pandas": lung_pd,
    "polars": lung_pl,
    "pyarrow": lung_pa,
    "duckdb": duckdb.from_arrow(lung_pa),
    "lazy": lung_pl.lazy(),
}

# The same call on each backend, and the coefficients agree exactly
for name, frame in frames.items():
    cox = gw.CoxPH().fit("Surv(time, status) ~ age + sex", data=frame)
    print(f"{name:8}", cox.coef_)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
pandas   [ 0.01704533 -0.51321852]
polars   [ 0.01704533 -0.51321852]
pyarrow  [ 0.01704533 -0.51321852]
duckdb   [ 0.01704533 -0.51321852]
lazy     [ 0.01704533 -0.51321852]
```
:::
:::


An `Outcome` works the same way, so the Kaplan-Meier curve below is identical whichever frame it
reads. With a lazy source, only the columns the call names are read, and they are collected once.

::: {#4395f488 .cell execution_count=7}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# The Outcome defined above, evaluated against a DuckDB relation
gw.KaplanMeier().fit(death, by="sex", data=frames["duckdb"])
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=7}
```
KaplanMeier (Kaplan-Meier survival estimate)

         n  events  median  0.95LCL  0.95UCL
sex=1  138     112     270      212      310
sex=2   90      53     426      348      550
```
:::
:::


You can choose whichever backend fits your existing workflow. If your pipeline already uses Polars,
stick with Polars. If it uses Pandas, stick with Pandas. The choice has no effect on any numerical
result.

::: {.callout-tip}
## Reading from files and databases

Greenwood does not read files itself, and it does not need to. Use your DataFrame library's reader
(`pandas.read_csv()`, `polars.scan_parquet()`, a database query, and so on) and pass the result as
`data=`. Anything that produces a supported DataFrame is a valid source, including lazy queries.
:::

## Shaping raw study data for a response

Real study exports often arrive in a form that needs light transformation before the times and event
indicators are ready. Three situations come up often: (1) the data stores calendar dates rather than
durations, (2) the event indicator is encoded in a compound or string status column, and (3) a
multi-endpoint DataFrame mixes rows that belong to different analyses. The first two are handled by
expressions inside the response, so the frame itself needs no changes.

### Computing durations from timestamps

The most common transformation is computing the elapsed time between two date columns. Write it as
`duration(start, end, unit=)` in the response. The outcome column needs no recoding either, since a
comparison says which value marks the event.

::: {#aaf9843e .cell execution_count=8}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import polars as pl

# Build a simulated study export with enrollment dates, exit dates, and outcomes
study = pl.DataFrame({
    "subject": [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6],
    "enroll":  ["2021-03-01", "2021-03-15", "2021-04-01",
                "2021-04-20", "2021-05-01", "2021-06-01"],
    "exit":    ["2022-01-10", "2021-09-22", "2023-01-01",
                "2021-12-05", "2022-08-14", "2022-03-30"],
    "outcome": ["died", "censored", "censored", "died", "withdrew", "died"],
}).with_columns(pl.col("enroll", "exit").str.to_date())

# The time is the gap between the two dates in days, and only "died" is an event
visit = gw.Outcome.surv(time="duration(enroll, exit)", event="outcome == 'died'")

# Fit a Kaplan-Meier curve straight from the export
gw.KaplanMeier().fit(visit, data=study)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=8}
```
KaplanMeier (Kaplan-Meier survival estimate)

     n  events  median  0.95LCL  0.95UCL
all  6       3     315      302       NA
```
:::
:::


A few things to notice in this example. First, the `outcome` column uses three values: `"died"` (the
event), `"censored"` (lost to follow-up at the exit date), and `"withdrew"` (left the study early).
With `outcome == 'died'`, the event is recorded only for `"died"`, and all other outcomes become
censored observations. Second, the exit date serves the same role regardless of the reason for exit:
it is always the last time at which the subject was observed, and the event column records what
happened at that time. `duration()` takes a `unit=` of `"days"` (the default), `"weeks"`,
`"months"`, or `"years"` (months and years use average lengths of 30.4375 and 365.25 days), and the
date columns can be dates, datetimes, or ISO date strings. In a formula, the same response is
`Surv(duration(enroll, exit), outcome == 'died')`.

::: {.callout-note}
## Missing values

Like R, a response keeps missing times and statuses, and estimators drop those rows when they fit.
With a formula or an `Outcome`, every row with a missing value in any column the model uses
(response, covariates, or grouping) is dropped once, and the printout reports how many.
:::

### Encoding from compound status columns

Some datasets store multiple outcome levels in a single integer status column. `pbc` is a typical
example: `status` is `0` (censored), `1` (transplant), or `2` (dead). The right encoding depends on
the analysis you intend to run, and each choice is one comparison. Do not pass this column raw.
Following R, a status whose largest value is `2` is read as a `1`/`2` coding, so the `0` rows would
become missing.

::: {#9e79e058 .cell execution_count=9}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load the pbc dataset, whose status column has three levels (0, 1, 2)
pbc = gw.load_dataset("pbc", backend="polars")

# All-cause analysis: anything other than 0 is an event
any_event = gw.Outcome.surv(time="time", event="status != 0")

# Death-only: transplant becomes censoring (patient still alive at last contact)
death_only = gw.Outcome.surv(time="time", event="status == 2")

# Compare event counts between the two endpoint definitions
print("all-cause: ", any_event.bind(pbc))
print("death-only:", death_only.bind(pbc))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
all-cause:  Surv(type=right, n=418, events=186)
death-only: Surv(type=right, n=418, events=161)
```
:::
:::


The event counts differ because 25 subjects reached transplant rather than death: the all-cause
endpoint treats them as events, the death-only endpoint treats them as censored. Which is correct
depends entirely on your research question. For a competing-risks analysis that keeps the two
outcomes separate, R's `factor()` turns the codes into states, with the first label meaning
censored:

::: {#cbcf240e .cell execution_count=10}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Transplant and death as competing states
gw.Outcome.surv(
    time="time",
    event="factor(status, c(0, 1, 2), c('censor', 'transplant', 'death'))",
).bind(pbc)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=10}
```
Surv(type=mright, n=418, events=186, states=('transplant', 'death'))
```
:::
:::


See the [competing risks](competing-risks.qmd) page for the analysis itself.

### Filtering multi-endpoint frames

Some study exports give each subject one row per potential endpoint. The `colon` dataset is
structured this way: `etype` is `1` for recurrence and `2` for death, so the full frame has 1,858
rows for 929 patients. Mixing rows from different endpoints in a single response is meaningless, so
always filter to one endpoint first, with your data frame library.

::: {#1d43d411 .cell execution_count=11}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load the colon dataset; each patient contributes two rows, one per endpoint
colon = gw.load_dataset("colon", backend="polars")

# Keep only the recurrence-endpoint rows
recurrence = colon.filter(pl.col("etype") == 1)

# Bind the response to the filtered frame (929 patients, not 1858 rows)
gw.Outcome.surv(time="time", event="status").bind(recurrence)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=11}
```
Surv(type=right, n=929, events=468)
```
:::
:::


The same principle applies to `mgus2`, which stores its two competing endpoints in separate column
pairs (`ptime`/`pstat` for progression, `futime`/`death` for death) rather than separate rows. For a
single endpoint, pick the matching pair. For a competing-risks analysis,
`gw.Outcome.first_event()` combines the pairs: the earliest observed event becomes the cause, ties go
to the endpoint listed first, and subjects with no event are censored at their last follow-up time.

::: {#9741fe4b .cell execution_count=12}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load the mgus2 competing-risks dataset
mgus2 = gw.load_dataset("mgus2", backend="polars")

# One (time, event) pair per endpoint, in priority order for ties
pcm_or_death = gw.Outcome.first_event(
    endpoints={"pcm": ("ptime", "pstat"), "death": ("futime", "death")}
)
pcm_or_death.bind(mgus2)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=12}
```
Surv(type=mright, n=1384, events=975, states=('pcm', 'death'))
```
:::
:::


## Tidy output frames

Every estimator and model returns its results as tidy data frames, through the `to_frame()` method
and through the `tidy()` and `glance()` functions. Pass `format=` (`"pandas"`, `"polars"`, or
`"pyarrow"`) to choose the backend; when you leave it off, Greenwood auto-detects one (Polars if
installed, otherwise Pandas, otherwise PyArrow). This makes the results easy to inspect, filter,
join, or pass on to a plotting or table library.

::: {#0d2f4c4a .cell execution_count=13}
``` {.python .cell-code}
# Load data and fit a Cox model with three covariates
lung = gw.load_dataset("lung", backend="polars")
cox = gw.CoxPH().fit("Surv(time, status) ~ age + sex + ph.ecog", data=lung)

# Retrieve the one-row model summary: n, events, log-likelihood, AIC
gw.glance(cox, format="polars")
```

