### `pbcseq`: Mayo Clinic PBC, sequential laboratory data

`pbcseq` is the companion to `pbc`. It holds the follow-up laboratory values of the 312 patients in
the randomized trial, with one row per clinic visit (1945 rows in all). Visits were scheduled at six
months, one year, and yearly after that, with extra visits when a patient's condition changed. Each
row repeats the patient's final follow-up time and status, so the frame is ready to turn into
counting-process data.

**Columns:** `id` (case number, as in `pbc`), `futime` (days to death, transplant, or the end of the
study), `status` (`0` = censored, `1` = transplant, `2` = dead), `trt`, `age`, `sex`, `day` (days
from enrollment to this visit), and the visit's measurements: `ascites`, `hepato`, `spiders`,
`edema`, `bili`, `chol`, `albumin`, `alk.phos`, `ast`, `platelet`, `protime`, and `stage`.

**Model fit:** `pbcseq` is the dataset for Cox models with time-varying covariates. The
`split_episodes()` function turns the visits into `(start, stop]` intervals, each carrying the lab
values measured at its start, and `CoxPH` fits them with a counting-process response. The
[Time-varying covariates](time-varying-covariates.qmd) page builds its examples on this dataset.
The `status` column uses the same three levels as `pbc`, so death alone is `status == 2`.

